text:
zoe: quick heads up - iOS quickstart (docs/quickstart-ios.md) has multiple small drifts that caused our sample-app CI harness to fail on the simulator this morning.

mike: what kind of drift?

zoe: three things piled up: 
1) README suggests adding the token to Info.plist as REDWOOD_API_KEY, but the SDK now expects the token via the new Redwood.configure(token:) call or via environment for simulator runs using REDWOOD_TOKEN.
2) the Swift snippet uses `RedwoodClient.shared.generate(prompt:)` but API now is `RedwoodClient.shared.completions.create(request:)` returning a Completion object.
3) the sample app's Podfile pins `pod 'redwood-ios-sdk', '~> 0.2'` which no longer matches package name `pod 'Redwood'` after the 1.0 release.

anya: ouch. the harness shows `missing token` then `unrecognized selector` errors depending on which change raced in. simulator runs were super confusing.

mike: can you paste the broken README snippet?

zoe: broken snippet:```swift
import UIKit

let token = Bundle.main.infoDictionary?["REDWOOD_API_KEY"] as? String
let client = RedwoodClient.shared
client.generate(prompt: "hello") { result in
  print(result.text)
}
```

anya: i see. since we moved to Completion API and a new configure pattern, that snippet fails at compile time for many users and at runtime for token extraction.

mike: propose corrected snippet and a short migration note. i'll open the PR.

zoe: suggested fix:```swift
import UIKit

// AppDelegate.swift - recommended pattern
import Redwood

@main
class AppDelegate: UIResponder, UIApplicationDelegate {
  func application(_ application: UIApplication, didFinishLaunchingWithOptions launchOptions: [UIApplication.LaunchOptionsKey: Any]?) -> Bool {
    // For simulator/local dev: set env or use a secret manager. Prefer runtime configure.
    if let token = ProcessInfo.processInfo.environment["REDWOOD_TOKEN"] {
      Redwood.configure(token: token)
    }
    return true
  }
}

// usage
let request = CompletionRequest(prompt: "hello")
Task {
  do {
    let completion = try await RedwoodClient.shared.completions.create(request: request)
    print(completion.text)
  } catch {
    print("completion failed:\\(error)")
  }
}
```

anya: nice. can we also add an explicit note showing how to pass env to the iOS simulator (`xcrun simctl spawn booted launchctl setenv REDWOOD_TOKEN <token>`) and a one-liner for Xcode scheme envs? new mobile devs kept pasting Info.plist changes and then wondered why the token wasn't populated in simulator runs.

mike: yes adding both. i'll also update the Podfile example to show `pod 'Redwood', '~> 1.0'` and add a migration blurb: "pod name and API changed in v1.0; prefer Redwood.configure(token:) and Completion API."

zoe: can we add a tiny unit test snippet runner for the Swift snippets? our snippet-checker doesn't run mobile code yet; it would be great to at least run a Swift lint/compile step using `swift build` in a minimal Package.swift harness so we catch compile-time API drift.

ci-bot: opened issue #1102 to add Swift snippet compile checks to docs CI. PRs that change quickstart-ios.md will trigger the new check once issue is closed.

mike: opened PR #993 to update quickstart-ios.md: fixes token handling guidance, updates sample to Completion API, updates Podfile, and adds simulator env instructions. added a short migration note in the iOS section.

docs-bot: PR #993 passed markdown lint and snippet-checker (non-compile checks). Follow-up issue #1102 created for Swift compile check.

anya: thanks — this should cut down a surprising amount of onboarding confusion for mobile folks.

zoe: thanks all! i'll follow up to add an example for Kotlin/Android too since we had a parallel drift there earlier. :sparkles:
